copt, BCC's peephole optimizer, as this port uses it to make bcc-cc1 small
enough for the 8086's 64K of code, and as cc -O uses it.

rules.86: Dev86's rules for 8086 code miscompile bcc-cc1 (it reported
"compiler bug? - bad address" when built with them). Every rule was checked
against the code BCC generates, and those that can change what a program
does were removed or narrowed. The hazards found: a rule that drops a load
into a register that later code still uses (BX, SI and DI carry pointers
and switch values past the lines a rule sees), so such rules now apply
only to AX, CX and DX, or to a global operand [_name]; an operand that may
be the register being loaded (mov bx,[bx]); a stack adjustment moved
across an instruction that may be a pop or a ret; std ... cld sequences;
a label renamed or moved (the jmp .1 / jmp .2 rule drops a label's dot,
and the strcpy rule moves a label and takes SI and DI for itself).
Copying a byte between two register pointers (*d++ = *s++) becomes
lodsb/stosb, as one of Dev86's rules did for one order of the increments.

rules.mc, new: for code compiled with callersaves (bcc-cc1 -c).

